<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lê Thái Tông</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1423">1423</a>–<a href="/years/1442">1442</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Le Thai Tong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lê Thái Tông was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> of the Later Lê dynasty from 1433 to 1442, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> as a boy. He died suddenly in 1442 after visiting the estate of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-trai">Nguyễn Trãi</a>, who was blamed and executed with his family, a case later recognised as an injustice.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Lê Nguyên Long on 22 December 1423, he was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> and lost his mother young. When Lê Lợi fell ill in 1433 he summoned his closest advisers, among them Lê Sát, Trịnh Khả and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-trai">Nguyễn Trãi</a>, to name the ten-year-old as heir. On Lê Lợi's death Lê Sát became regent. He governed more for himself than for the boy, removing many rivals, and the young emperor turned for support to Trịnh Khả, whom Lê Sát had sent into exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On taking full power in 1438 Thái Tông recalled Trịnh Khả and made him head of the Palace Guards over Lê Sát's objections. A few months later Lê Sát was accused of usurping the emperor's power, tried and executed. In spring 1440 the emperor personally led a campaign against the chief Hà Tông Lai in Tuyên Quang, who was beheaded within a week. Later that year another chief, Nghiễm, rose in Gia Hưng prefecture and submitted, and when he rebelled again in 1441 the emperor's third campaign defeated a Laotian army sent to aid him. The court annalist Vũ Quỳnh later called him a heroic emperor.</p><p>His court filled with intrigue as his affections moved from one consort to another. His first wife was a daughter of Lê Sát and his second a daughter of Le Ngan, while Duong thi Bi bore his first son, Nghi Dân. Nguyễn Thị Anh bore his third son and heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-nhan-tong">Lê Nhân Tông</a>, and Ngo Thi Ngoc Dao the later emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 4 August 1442 the emperor visited the Lệ Chi Viên, or Lychee Garden, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-trai">Nguyễn Trãi</a> at Đại Lai in Bắc Ninh Province, where Trãi's concubine Lady Nguyễn Thị Lộ attended him. He fell suddenly ill and died. Trãi was accused of killing him and executed with his family. In 1464 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a> proclaimed Trãi wholly innocent and gave office to his surviving son, Nguyễn Anh Vũ.</p><p>Opinion remained divided: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-quy-don">Lê Quý Đôn</a> in the 18th century held that Trãi should not be counted a meritorious official, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-si-lien">Ngô Sĩ Liên</a> and Phan Huy Chú suggested that only Lady Lộ might be guilty, and other scholars think she was a victim of Nguyễn Thị Anh or that the emperor died naturally.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lê Thái Tông” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.02,105.84&amp;year=1435" aria-label="Hanoi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7396 1386 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lê Thái Tông"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.02,105.84&amp;year=1435">What was here in 1435? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
